{"parentUuid":"2cbce9df-de89-461a-8839-7259dfda7714","isSidechain":false,"userType":"external","cwd":"/Users/sa/rh.1","sessionId":"94aabe90-9b2d-48e7-be8c-6bf820a0b6a9","version":"2.1.59","gitBranch":"stage4-release","slug":"snazzy-skipping-brook","type":"progress","data":{"message":{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YX8LS2jkeqWkaULrH6Zofj","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"Play to Xcode\" SDK dependencies Package.swift structure\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Adding Swift package dependencies in Xcode - a free Hacking with iOS: SwiftUI Edition tutorial\",\"url\":\"https://www.hackingwithswift.com/books/ios-swiftui/adding-swift-package-dependencies-in-xcode\"},{\"title\":\"Adding and Removing Swift Package dependencies in Xcode | Sarunw\",\"url\":\"https://sarunw.com/posts/managing-swift-package-in-xcode/\"},{\"title\":\"Managing dependencies using the Swift Package Manager | Swift by Sundell\",\"url\":\"https://www.swiftbysundell.com/articles/managing-dependencies-using-the-swift-package-manager/\"},{\"title\":\"How to Add and Remove Package Dependencies in Xcode | by Ijeoma Nelson | iOSPlayBook | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://medium.com/iosplaybook/how-to-add-and-remove-package-dependencies-in-xcode-e235b38265d3\"},{\"title\":\"Managing package dependencies with Swift Package Manager (SwiftPM) in Xcode · Alexander Sandberg\",\"url\":\"https://alexandersandberg.com/articles/managing-package-dependencies-with-swift-package-manager-in-xcode/\"},{\"title\":\"Adding package dependencies to your app | Apple Developer Documentation\",\"url\":\"https://developer.apple.com/documentation/xcode/adding-package-dependencies-to-your-app\"},{\"title\":\"Creating a standalone Swift package with Xcode | Apple Developer Documentation\",\"url\":\"https://developer.apple.com/documentation/xcode/creating-a-standalone-swift-package-with-xcode\"},{\"title\":\"Package dependency in own package - Package Manager - Swift Forums\",\"url\":\"https://forums.swift.org/t/package-dependency-in-own-package/64587\"},{\"title\":\"How to Add a Swift Package to a Project\",\"url\":\"https://cocoacasts.com/xcode-fundamentals-how-to-add-a-swift-package-to-a-project\"},{\"title\":\"Adding a Swift package to your app playground | Apple Developer Documentation\",\"url\":\"https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swift-playgrounds/add-a-swift-package\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's information about SDK dependencies and Package.swift structure in Xcode:\n\n## Package.swift Structure\n\nThe contents of a package is declared using a Package.swift manifest file, which is placed in the root directory of each package. A package contains Swift source files and a manifest file, known as Package.swift. Dependencies are modules required by the code inside the package.\n\n## SDK Dependencies\n\nA dependency has either a relative or absolute URL to the source of the package; and requirements for the package version. One of the Swift Package Manager's core use cases is enabling remote dependencies — such as third party libraries — to be added to a project. Any package that can be fetched through Git can be added simply by specifying its URL, as well as the version constraint that we wish to apply to it.\n\n## Managing Dependencies in Xcode\n\nTo add a package dependency to your Xcode project, select File > Add Packages... and enter a repository URL of a package you want to add in the search field on the top right. Xcode automatically fetches the Swift package and adds the package products to the targets.\n\nThe search results provide general information about Swift Package Manager and dependencies, but don't contain specific details about the \"Play to Xcode\" SDK. If you're looking for documentation specific to that SDK, you may want to search for its official documentation or repository.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"28047f69-dee6-4914-9b36-f482ad02628f","timestamp":"2026-02-28T15:28:40.218Z"},"normalizedMessages":[],"type":"agent_progress","prompt":"Research Play.app (createwithplay.com / play.app) — the iOS native design tool that exports SwiftUI. I need to know:\n\n1. What UI component library does Play provide? (cards, buttons, navigation, lists, etc.)\n2. How does the SwiftUI export work — what format, any dependencies?\n3. Can Play components be used in a Swift Playground App (.swiftpm) project directly?\n4. Any known limitations of Play's exported SwiftUI code?\n5. Does Play have a package/framework that can be added as a dependency, or is it purely copy-paste export?\n\nFocus on practical integration details, not marketing. Return concise findings.","agentId":"ac81190be71e37ddb"},"toolUseID":"agent_msg_013RyFL7KeXnhue2VoKZaSY3","parentToolUseID":"toolu_01VqFLFYAU1sv1chDcEPiXGY","uuid":"942b1251-6a13-4c5a-b96d-583c6218f37f","timestamp":"2026-02-28T15:28:40.219Z"}
{"parentUuid":"942b1251-6a13-4c5a-b96d-583c6218f37f","isSidechain":false,"userType":"external","cwd":"/Users/sa/rh.1","sessionId":"94aabe90-9b2d-48e7-be8c-6bf820a0b6a9","version":"2.1.59","gitBranch":"stage4-release","slug":"snazzy-skipping-brook","type":"progress","data":{"message":{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XUubigi4fy3xkxDAAVvhQ1","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Play app iOS limitations exported code UIKit SwiftUI dependencies\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"GitHub - CreateWithPlayApp/PlaySDK\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/CreateWithPlayApp/PlaySDK\"},{\"title\":\"How Play to Xcode Simplifies iOS App Design-to-Development - Geeky Gadgets\",\"url\":\"https://www.geeky-gadgets.com/play-to-xcode-export-process/\"},{\"title\":\"Exporting SwiftUI Code | Play's Documentation\",\"url\":\"https://learn.createwithplay.com/en/articles/8500289-exporting-swiftui-code\"},{\"title\":\"Swift Playgrounds App Projects | Aaron Sky\",\"url\":\"https://skyaaron.com/posts/swiftpm-app-projects/\"},{\"title\":\"Play for iOS Engineers - Play · Design mobile apps with the power of iOS & SwiftUI\",\"url\":\"https://createwithplay.com/blog/play-for-engineers\"},{\"title\":\"Use SwiftUI with UIKit - WWDC22 - Videos - Apple Developer\",\"url\":\"https://developer.apple.com/videos/play/wwdc2022/10072/\"},{\"title\":\"SwiftUI - Apple Developer\",\"url\":\"https://developer.apple.com/swiftui/\"},{\"title\":\"Modularizing iOS Applications with SwiftUI and Swift Package Manager - A Modern Approach – Nimble\",\"url\":\"https://nimblehq.co/blog/modern-approach-modularize-ios-swiftui-spm\"},{\"title\":\"Managing dependencies using the Swift Package Manager | Swift by Sundell\",\"url\":\"https://www.swiftbysundell.com/articles/managing-dependencies-using-the-swift-package-manager/\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - pointfreeco/swift-dependencies: A dependency management library inspired by SwiftUI's \\\"environment.\\\"\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/pointfreeco/swift-dependencies\"}]\n\nHere are the search results for your query about Play app iOS limitations with exported code, UIKit, SwiftUI, and dependencies:\n\n## Key Findings:\n\n**Play App Export Capabilities:**\nThe Play to Xcode feature allows seamless export of designs, components, and assets from Play into Xcode as a Swift package, supporting both SwiftUI and UIKit frameworks while preserving animations and interactions.\n\n**Technical Requirements:**\nThe SDK supports a minimum iOS version of 16.0+, uses Swift Tools Version 5.10 and does not yet support Swift 6.\n\n**SwiftUI Code Export Limitations:**\nWhen copying laid out views, interactions will not be brought into Xcode, and when interaction code is pasted into Xcode, the UI design from Play will NOT be brought over; instead, the simulator displays a placeholder object.\n\n**Framework Support:**\nYou can select your preferred framework: SwiftUI or UIKit, depending on your project's requirements.\n\n**Dependencies & Integration:**\nPlay will automatically add the necessary packages and entitlements to your existing project. Additionally, essential assets such as fonts, images, and videos are included in the Swift package to ensure full integration of design elements.\n\nThe search results focus primarily on Play's design-to-code export capabilities rather than specific limitations with framework dependencies, but they show that Play supports both UIKit and SwiftUI frameworks for iOS 16.0 and above.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"f1131b7b-575d-4524-90e8-e51fd288a5f1","timestamp":"2026-02-28T15:28:40.311Z"},"normalizedMessages":[],"type":"agent_progress","prompt":"Research Play.app (createwithplay.com / play.app) — the iOS native design tool that exports SwiftUI. I need to know:\n\n1. What UI component library does Play provide? (cards, buttons, navigation, lists, etc.)\n2. How does the SwiftUI export work — what format, any dependencies?\n3. Can Play components be used in a Swift Playground App (.swiftpm) project directly?\n4. Any known limitations of Play's exported SwiftUI code?\n5. Does Play have a package/framework that can be added as a dependency, or is it purely copy-paste export?\n\nFocus on practical integration details, not marketing. Return concise findings.","agentId":"ac81190be71e37ddb"},"toolUseID":"agent_msg_013RyFL7KeXnhue2VoKZaSY3","parentToolUseID":"toolu_01VqFLFYAU1sv1chDcEPiXGY","uuid":"9ad5299e-7d6a-4e3d-bd5d-be104abf0cbc","timestamp":"2026-02-28T15:28:40.312Z"}
{"parentUuid":"9ad5299e-7d6a-4e3d-bd5d-be104abf0cbc","isSidechain":false,"userType":"external","cwd":"/Users/sa/rh.1","sessionId":"94aabe90-9b2d-48e7-be8c-6bf820a0b6a9","version":"2.1.59","gitBranch":"stage4-release","slug":"snazzy-skipping-brook","type":"progress","data":{"message":{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01T4WVG74ZxLsBjyjrbzMi2w","type":"tool_result","content":"# PlaySDK Overview\n\nBased on the provided documentation, here's what I found:\n\n## Components\n\nThe README mentions **Chip** as an example component but doesn't provide a comprehensive list of included components. It states that \"The PlaySDK makes all of your Play styles, components, assets, views, and pages accessible inside Xcode,\" suggesting components are exported from the Play design application rather than predefined in the SDK itself.\n\n## Dependencies\n\nThe documentation doesn't explicitly list external dependencies. However, it notes the SDK requirements:\n- \"Minimum iOS version of **16.0+**\"\n- \"Tested and developed using Xcode **16.4**\"\n- \"Uses Swift Tools Version **5.10** and does not yet support Swift 6\"\n\n## Structure\n\nPlaySDK appears to be organized as a Swift Package Manager project with:\n- `Sources/PlaySDKWrapper` directory (visible in the file tree)\n- Support for both SwiftUI and UIKit integration\n- Components that expose state management via `.state()` methods and `.onStateChange` callbacks\n\nThe SDK functions primarily as a **wrapper** around exported Play designs, making them usable within existing Xcode projects rather than providing a pre-built component library."}]},"uuid":"740d818d-2f8d-46e0-8ce0-902078b7c7d0","timestamp":"2026-02-28T15:28:42.439Z"},"normalizedMessages":[],"type":"agent_progress","prompt":"Research Play.app (createwithplay.com / play.app) — the iOS native design tool that exports SwiftUI. I need to know:\n\n1. What UI component library does Play provide? (cards, buttons, navigation, lists, etc.)\n2. How does the SwiftUI export work — what format, any dependencies?\n3. Can Play components be used in a Swift Playground App (.swiftpm) project directly?\n4. Any known limitations of Play's exported SwiftUI code?\n5. Does Play have a package/framework that can be added as a dependency, or is it purely copy-paste export?\n\nFocus on practical integration details, not marketing. Return concise findings.","agentId":"ac81190be71e37ddb"},"toolUseID":"agent_msg_013RyFL7KeXnhue2VoKZaSY3","parentToolUseID":"toolu_01VqFLFYAU1sv1chDcEPiXGY","uuid":"b8bdd244-30ac-420c-92a0-07d0e10dd3e8","timestamp":"2026-02-28T15:28:42.440Z"}
