{"parentUuid":"7f6b59d7-d4ba-4a56-8d9e-7217fff2522e","isSidechain":false,"userType":"external","cwd":"/Users/sa/rh.1","sessionId":"94aabe90-9b2d-48e7-be8c-6bf820a0b6a9","version":"2.1.59","gitBranch":"stage4-release","slug":"snazzy-skipping-brook","type":"progress","data":{"message":{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UZHdrEsPenKeLKzMUq8GFi","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Play app design tool component list buttons cards forms inputs\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Play · Design and ship mobile apps with the power of iOS & SwiftUI\",\"url\":\"https://createwithplay.com/\"},{\"title\":\"Intro to Play - Design and Prototype an App with Play - Design+Code\",\"url\":\"https://designcode.io/play-intro/\"},{\"title\":\"Layout and Components - Design and Prototype an App with Play - Design+Code\",\"url\":\"https://designcode.io/play-layout-components/\"},{\"title\":\"Play puts native iOS product design tool in the palm of your hand | TechCrunch\",\"url\":\"https://techcrunch.com/2021/11/01/play-puts-native-ios-product-design-tool-in-the-palm-of-your-hand/\"},{\"title\":\"Button Component | Thunkable Docs\",\"url\":\"https://docs.thunkable.com/app-design/ui-components/basic-components/button\"},{\"title\":\"Card UI design: fundamentals and examples - Justinmind\",\"url\":\"https://www.justinmind.com/ui-design/cards\"},{\"title\":\"Card UI Design: Best practices, Design variants & Examples | Mobbin — UI & UX design inspiration for mobile & web apps\",\"url\":\"https://mobbin.com/glossary/card\"},{\"title\":\"Card UI Design: The Best Practices and 25 Best Examples\",\"url\":\"https://www.mockplus.com/blog/post/card-ui-design\"},{\"title\":\"17 Card UI Design Examples and Best Practices for Your Inspiration\",\"url\":\"https://www.eleken.co/blog-posts/card-ui-examples-and-best-practices-for-product-owners\"},{\"title\":\"Play: Create Better Apps App - App Store\",\"url\":\"https://apps.apple.com/us/app/play-create-better-apps/id6669873480\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about the Play app design tool and its component list:\n\n## Play App Design Tool\n\nPlay offers access to common user interface components like buttons, cards and video players, as well as Page Layouts for creating mobile app designs.\n\n### Available Components\n\nPlay's features include design with native iOS elements like Apple Maps, Swift Charts, SF Symbols, Materials, input text fields, date pickers, switches, steppers, modals, and more.\n\nYou can design on a freeform canvas with access to real native elements like maps, pickers, and input text fields.\n\n### Key Capabilities\n\nPlay takes a new approach to app design by giving you access to the native materials of the medium, but design and prototype from both your Mac and iPhone.\n\nYou can export styles, components, full pages & flows directly to Xcode, making it useful for iOS developers.\n\nDesigns and prototypes are responsive by default in Play, and you can easily view your work across every iPhone device with the tap of a button.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"9fad657a-91b1-468c-a1a4-44cd68be622d","timestamp":"2026-02-28T15:28:51.295Z"},"normalizedMessages":[],"type":"agent_progress","prompt":"Research Play.app (createwithplay.com / play.app) — the iOS native design tool that exports SwiftUI. I need to know:\n\n1. What UI component library does Play provide? (cards, buttons, navigation, lists, etc.)\n2. How does the SwiftUI export work — what format, any dependencies?\n3. Can Play components be used in a Swift Playground App (.swiftpm) project directly?\n4. Any known limitations of Play's exported SwiftUI code?\n5. Does Play have a package/framework that can be added as a dependency, or is it purely copy-paste export?\n\nFocus on practical integration details, not marketing. Return concise findings.","agentId":"ac81190be71e37ddb"},"toolUseID":"agent_msg_013RyFL7KeXnhue2VoKZaSY3","parentToolUseID":"toolu_01VqFLFYAU1sv1chDcEPiXGY","uuid":"64b685c4-9516-4bf1-b52c-28ba348f7a14","timestamp":"2026-02-28T15:28:51.297Z"}
{"parentUuid":"64b685c4-9516-4bf1-b52c-28ba348f7a14","isSidechain":false,"userType":"external","cwd":"/Users/sa/rh.1","sessionId":"94aabe90-9b2d-48e7-be8c-6bf820a0b6a9","version":"2.1.59","gitBranch":"stage4-release","slug":"snazzy-skipping-brook","type":"progress","data":{"message":{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Vc3r2bcKN9QdxfG7kEurHD","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"Play.app\" \"copy-paste\" export versus SDK framework approach\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Copy and paste | Views | Android Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developer.android.com/develop/ui/views/touch-and-input/copy-paste\"},{\"title\":\"Copy and Paste | Android Developers\",\"url\":\"https://stuff.mit.edu/afs/sipb/project/android/docs/guide/topics/text/copy-paste.html\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - CreateWithPlayApp/PlaySDK\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/CreateWithPlayApp/PlaySDK\"},{\"title\":\"Android - Clipboard Manager with Example - GeeksforGeeks\",\"url\":\"https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/android-clipboard-manager-with-example/\"},{\"title\":\"Android Clipboard - Copy and Paste Text Example in Kotlin - EyeHunts\",\"url\":\"https://tutorial.eyehunts.com/android/android-clipboard-copy-paste-text/\"},{\"title\":\"Android - Clipboard\",\"url\":\"https://www.tutorialspoint.com/android/android_clipboard.htm\"},{\"title\":\"Working with Clipboard in Android | by Pius Aboyi | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://medium.com/@aboyi/working-with-clipboard-in-android-985a5806c81b\"},{\"title\":\"Export SDK to Xcode - Createwithplay\",\"url\":\"https://docs.createwithplay.com/en/articles/export-sdk\"},{\"title\":\"Copy and Paste | Android Developers\",\"url\":\"https://spot.pcc.edu/~mgoodman/developer.android.com/guide/topics/text/copy-paste.html\"},{\"title\":\"Copy and paste | Jetpack Compose | Android Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developer.android.com/develop/ui/compose/touch-input/copy-and-paste\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found relevant information about Play.app's export approaches. Here's what the search revealed:\n\n## Play.app Export Approaches\n\nPlay.app allows you to design and build apps inside the Play app, export their work, integrate it into their existing codebase, and ship it to the App Store using their SDK framework approach.\n\n### SDK Framework Approach\n\nPlay's SDK enables users to export their Play project to Xcode. The SDK method offers flexibility with two main pathways:\n\n1. **New Projects**: Users can select \"New Xcode Project\" and choose if the Swift package should be SwiftUI or UIKit.\n\n2. **Existing Projects**: During the export process, Play gives the option to add the project to an existing app by hitting Publish, then Export New Project, and selecting an existing Xcode project, which Play will automatically add the necessary packages and entitlements to.\n\n### Key Features\n\nOnce you've exported your Play project to Xcode, you can continuously update the package with new changes made to your design in Play, providing ongoing synchronization between the design tool and code.\n\nThe search results focused primarily on the SDK framework approach rather than a \"copy-paste\" export method, suggesting that Play.app's primary export strategy emphasizes the SDK integration model for more maintainable and updatable code integration.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"d0a5b1ad-9fc6-4a59-a8bd-03dd4d5c4a49","timestamp":"2026-02-28T15:28:52.921Z"},"normalizedMessages":[],"type":"agent_progress","prompt":"Research Play.app (createwithplay.com / play.app) — the iOS native design tool that exports SwiftUI. I need to know:\n\n1. What UI component library does Play provide? (cards, buttons, navigation, lists, etc.)\n2. How does the SwiftUI export work — what format, any dependencies?\n3. Can Play components be used in a Swift Playground App (.swiftpm) project directly?\n4. Any known limitations of Play's exported SwiftUI code?\n5. Does Play have a package/framework that can be added as a dependency, or is it purely copy-paste export?\n\nFocus on practical integration details, not marketing. Return concise findings.","agentId":"ac81190be71e37ddb"},"toolUseID":"agent_msg_013RyFL7KeXnhue2VoKZaSY3","parentToolUseID":"toolu_01VqFLFYAU1sv1chDcEPiXGY","uuid":"28f0a1ef-0d61-4456-a7ae-413a7f555134","timestamp":"2026-02-28T15:28:52.922Z"}
{"parentUuid":"28f0a1ef-0d61-4456-a7ae-413a7f555134","isSidechain":false,"userType":"external","cwd":"/Users/sa/rh.1","sessionId":"94aabe90-9b2d-48e7-be8c-6bf820a0b6a9","version":"2.1.59","gitBranch":"stage4-release","slug":"snazzy-skipping-brook","type":"progress","data":{"type":"hook_progress","hookEvent":"PostToolUse","hookName":"PostToolUse:Task","command":"entire hooks claude-code post-task"},"parentToolUseID":"toolu_01VqFLFYAU1sv1chDcEPiXGY","toolUseID":"toolu_01VqFLFYAU1sv1chDcEPiXGY","timestamp":"2026-02-28T15:29:17.646Z","uuid":"df784bc4-8f30-432d-8310-df0e0c4959e5"}
{"parentUuid":"e99e12ca-4a17-4150-9f2d-6cfac3720542","isSidechain":false,"userType":"external","cwd":"/Users/sa/rh.1","sessionId":"94aabe90-9b2d-48e7-be8c-6bf820a0b6a9","version":"2.1.59","gitBranch":"stage4-release","slug":"snazzy-skipping-brook","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VqFLFYAU1sv1chDcEPiXGY","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect. Now I have comprehensive information. Let me compile the findings with a focus on practical integration details.\n\n## Play.app Technical Research Findings\n\n### 1. **UI Component Library**\n\nPlay provides access to native iOS components rather than a custom design system library:\n- **Native controls**: Buttons, cards, input text fields, date pickers, switches, steppers, modals\n- **Advanced elements**: Apple Maps, Swift Charts, SF Symbols, Materials\n- **Form components**: Input fields, steppers, date/time pickers\n- **Media**: Video players\n\nComponents are **not predefined** — you design them in Play, and they're exported as part of your project output.\n\n---\n\n### 2. **SwiftUI Export Format**\n\nPlay offers **two distinct export pathways**:\n\n| Export Method | Format | Use Case |\n|---------------|--------|----------|\n| **Code Panel** | Copy-paste SwiftUI code snippet | Reference/learning; code-only (no interactions) |\n| **Play to Xcode SDK** | Complete Swift Package (.swiftpm/.spm) | Production; preserves components, styles, assets, animations, interactions |\n\n**Important limitation**: When you copy SwiftUI code from the Code Panel, interactions are not transferred. Conversely, if you only copy interaction code, the UI design is lost and replaced with placeholder objects.\n\n---\n\n### 3. **Swift Playground App (.swiftpm) Compatibility**\n\n**Yes, Play exports can be used in Swift Playground Apps.**\n\n- Play's \"Play to Xcode\" exports as a `.swiftpm` file (Swift Playground format)\n- Swift Playground Apps are backed by Swift Package Manager and support SwiftUI exclusively\n- Minimum iOS: **16.0+**\n- Swift Tools Version: **5.10** (no Swift 6 support yet)\n- Tested with Xcode **16.4**\n\nThe exported SDK integrates seamlessly with `.swiftpm` projects since both use the same SPM infrastructure.\n\n---\n\n### 4. **Known Limitations**\n\n1. **Code-only exports lack interactivity** — if you copy layout code, interactions don't transfer; if you copy interaction code, UI doesn't transfer\n2. **Swift 6 not yet supported** — SDK requires Swift 5.10\n3. **App Store notice**: Starting **April 20, 2026**, Play will discontinue iOS and macOS apps, though existing exports and SDKs will continue functioning\n4. **iOS 16+ only** — no support for earlier versions\n5. **Design preview loss in code export** — simulator shows placeholder objects when code-only is used\n\n---\n\n### 5. **Package/Framework vs. Copy-Paste**\n\n**Framework approach (recommended)**:\n- **PlaySDK** is a Swift Package available on GitHub: https://github.com/CreateWithPlayApp/PlaySDK\n- Exports as a full Swift package with all styles, components, assets, variables, and pages\n- Play automatically adds required packages and entitlements to your Xcode project\n- Supports both SwiftUI and UIKit frameworks\n- Allows **continuous updates** — modify designs in Play, re-export, and the package updates\n\n**Copy-paste approach (limited)**:\n- Code Panel provides copy-paste SwiftUI snippets for reference\n- **No dependencies** — pure SwiftUI code\n- **No interactions**, **no state management** — layout only\n- Not recommended for production\n\n---\n\n## Practical Integration Summary\n\n**For Swift Playground App projects**, use **Play to Xcode SDK** (not Code Panel copy-paste):\n1. Export from Play → select \"New Xcode Project\" or add to existing\n2. Choose SwiftUI framework\n3. Play generates `.swiftpm` package with PlaySDK wrapper\n4. Add the package to your Playground App project\n5. Import components and pages directly from the exported package\n\n**Zero custom dependency chains** — PlaySDK wraps your Play designs as native SwiftUI/UIKit without external third-party libraries.\n\n---\n\nSources:\n- [Play · Design and ship mobile apps](https://createwithplay.com/)\n- [Play for iOS Engineers](https://createwithplay.com/blog/play-for-engineers)\n- [PlaySDK GitHub Repository](https://github.com/CreateWithPlayApp/PlaySDK)\n- [Design+Code: Play Layout and Components](https://designcode.io/play-layout-components/)\n- [Swift Playgrounds App Projects](https://skyaaron.com/posts/swiftpm-app-projects/)"},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: ac81190be71e37ddb (for resuming to continue this agent's work if needed)\n<usage>total_tokens: 62742\ntool_uses: 12\nduration_ms: 100042</usage>"}]}]},"uuid":"b40d44b1-9fe3-4ebb-bc47-4b355f55d3ec","timestamp":"2026-02-28T15:29:17.645Z","toolUseResult":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Research Play.app (createwithplay.com / play.app) — the iOS native design tool that exports SwiftUI. I need to know:\n\n1. What UI component library does Play provide? (cards, buttons, navigation, lists, etc.)\n2. How does the SwiftUI export work — what format, any dependencies?\n3. Can Play components be used in a Swift Playground App (.swiftpm) project directly?\n4. Any known limitations of Play's exported SwiftUI code?\n5. Does Play have a package/framework that can be added as a dependency, or is it purely copy-paste export?\n\nFocus on practical integration details, not marketing. Return concise findings.","agentId":"ac81190be71e37ddb","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect. Now I have comprehensive information. Let me compile the findings with a focus on practical integration details.\n\n## Play.app Technical Research Findings\n\n### 1. **UI Component Library**\n\nPlay provides access to native iOS components rather than a custom design system library:\n- **Native controls**: Buttons, cards, input text fields, date pickers, switches, steppers, modals\n- **Advanced elements**: Apple Maps, Swift Charts, SF Symbols, Materials\n- **Form components**: Input fields, steppers, date/time pickers\n- **Media**: Video players\n\nComponents are **not predefined** — you design them in Play, and they're exported as part of your project output.\n\n---\n\n### 2. **SwiftUI Export Format**\n\nPlay offers **two distinct export pathways**:\n\n| Export Method | Format | Use Case |\n|---------------|--------|----------|\n| **Code Panel** | Copy-paste SwiftUI code snippet | Reference/learning; code-only (no interactions) |\n| **Play to Xcode SDK** | Complete Swift Package (.swiftpm/.spm) | Production; preserves components, styles, assets, animations, interactions |\n\n**Important limitation**: When you copy SwiftUI code from the Code Panel, interactions are not transferred. Conversely, if you only copy interaction code, the UI design is lost and replaced with placeholder objects.\n\n---\n\n### 3. **Swift Playground App (.swiftpm) Compatibility**\n\n**Yes, Play exports can be used in Swift Playground Apps.**\n\n- Play's \"Play to Xcode\" exports as a `.swiftpm` file (Swift Playground format)\n- Swift Playground Apps are backed by Swift Package Manager and support SwiftUI exclusively\n- Minimum iOS: **16.0+**\n- Swift Tools Version: **5.10** (no Swift 6 support yet)\n- Tested with Xcode **16.4**\n\nThe exported SDK integrates seamlessly with `.swiftpm` projects since both use the same SPM infrastructure.\n\n---\n\n### 4. **Known Limitations**\n\n1. **Code-only exports lack interactivity** — if you copy layout code, interactions don't transfer; if you copy interaction code, UI doesn't transfer\n2. **Swift 6 not yet supported** — SDK requires Swift 5.10\n3. **App Store notice**: Starting **April 20, 2026**, Play will discontinue iOS and macOS apps, though existing exports and SDKs will continue functioning\n4. **iOS 16+ only** — no support for earlier versions\n5. **Design preview loss in code export** — simulator shows placeholder objects when code-only is used\n\n---\n\n### 5. **Package/Framework vs. Copy-Paste**\n\n**Framework approach (recommended)**:\n- **PlaySDK** is a Swift Package available on GitHub: https://github.com/CreateWithPlayApp/PlaySDK\n- Exports as a full Swift package with all styles, components, assets, variables, and pages\n- Play automatically adds required packages and entitlements to your Xcode project\n- Supports both SwiftUI and UIKit frameworks\n- Allows **continuous updates** — modify designs in Play, re-export, and the package updates\n\n**Copy-paste approach (limited)**:\n- Code Panel provides copy-paste SwiftUI snippets for reference\n- **No dependencies** — pure SwiftUI code\n- **No interactions**, **no state management** — layout only\n- Not recommended for production\n\n---\n\n## Practical Integration Summary\n\n**For Swift Playground App projects**, use **Play to Xcode SDK** (not Code Panel copy-paste):\n1. Export from Play → select \"New Xcode Project\" or add to existing\n2. Choose SwiftUI framework\n3. Play generates `.swiftpm` package with PlaySDK wrapper\n4. Add the package to your Playground App project\n5. Import components and pages directly from the exported package\n\n**Zero custom dependency chains** — PlaySDK wraps your Play designs as native SwiftUI/UIKit without external third-party libraries.\n\n---\n\nSources:\n- [Play · Design and ship mobile apps](https://createwithplay.com/)\n- [Play for iOS Engineers](https://createwithplay.com/blog/play-for-engineers)\n- [PlaySDK GitHub Repository](https://github.com/CreateWithPlayApp/PlaySDK)\n- [Design+Code: Play Layout and Components](https://designcode.io/play-layout-components/)\n- [Swift Playgrounds App Projects](https://skyaaron.com/posts/swiftpm-app-projects/)"}],"totalDurationMs":100042,"totalTokens":62742,"totalToolUseCount":12,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3337,"cache_read_input_tokens":58360,"output_tokens":1038,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3337,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"e99e12ca-4a17-4150-9f2d-6cfac3720542"}
