{"parentUuid":"c4e0ec49-6a83-4a5c-b16b-f8d4d94d7558","isSidechain":false,"userType":"external","cwd":"/Users/sa/rh.1","sessionId":"94aabe90-9b2d-48e7-be8c-6bf820a0b6a9","version":"2.1.59","gitBranch":"stage4-release","slug":"snazzy-skipping-brook","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_018VEXXm8DeEdoRSb4uvCmh8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I can see the iPhone Mirroring window showing the home screen, and I can see \"Q Search\" at the bottom. My clicks are not registering properly on the mirrored phone. \n\nLet me think about this differently. The iPhone Mirroring window might have some internal padding or the content area might not start where I think it does. Also, iPhone Mirroring might handle input differently than a regular macOS window - the clicks might need to go through a specific interaction layer.\n\nLet me try a completely different approach:\n1. Try clicking in the middle of the phone screen first to make sure input is being accepted\n2. Try using keyboard shortcuts\n\nActually, I wonder if the issue is that iPhone Mirroring requires clicking directly on the rendered phone screen, and the title bar and window chrome take up space. Let me try to be more precise.\n\n\nLooking at the screenshot more carefully, the mirrored phone screen appears centered in the window with black padding around it, and I'm noticing what might be an App Library view on the right side with small app icons - which could mean one of my previous interactions actually worked and changed the view. Let me try clicking directly on a visible app icon in the middle of the phone content to confirm input is being registered.\n\nActually, I think I should step back and try a simpler approach - maybe just clicking in the absolute center of the mirroring window first to verify that clicks are working at all, or I could try using the keyboard to trigger Spotlight search since iPhone Mirroring supports keyboard input.","signature":""}],"