{"parentUuid":"b1ef9c55-9731-435a-a587-19aef7b28305","isSidechain":false,"userType":"external","cwd":"/Users/sa/rh.1","sessionId":"94aabe90-9b2d-48e7-be8c-6bf820a0b6a9","version":"2.1.59","gitBranch":"stage4-release","slug":"snazzy-skipping-brook","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01HAEZtbV8TKnAv8im3GAR8C","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Still no change on the mirrored phone screen. The clicks and keyboard input don't seem to be registering. \n\nThis could be a permissions issue. iPhone Mirroring might require accessibility permissions for cliclick to interact with it, or the mirroring window might intercept/handle input differently.\n\nLet me think about this:\n1. cliclick uses CGEventPost which creates system-level mouse events\n2. iPhone Mirroring might have a special rendering layer that doesn't respond to regular CGEvents\n3. There might be a macOS security restriction preventing programmatic input to iPhone Mirroring\n\nLet me check if cliclick has the accessibility permissions needed. Also, I should check if there's an alternative approach - maybe using AppleScript to directly send mouse events to the iPhone Mirroring app.\n\n\nI'm wondering if Apple's implemented some kind of secure rendering mode for iPhone Mirroring similar to DRM protection, which would block any programmatic interaction as a security measure. Let me try using AppleScript to target the iPhone Mirroring app directly instead of relying on system-level events, or maybe I should test if osascript can simulate clicks within the app itself.\n\nWriting AppleScript click simulation...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":430,"cache_read_input_tokens":96276,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":430},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"}},"requestId":"req_011CYara4F3SqtgH1R2G2XjV","type":"