{"parentUuid":"f5731026-df32-4c99-8541-7d85242a1037","isSidechain":false,"userType":"external","cwd":"/Users/sa/rh.1","sessionId":"94aabe90-9b2d-48e7-be8c-6bf820a0b6a9","version":"2.1.59","gitBranch":"stage4-release","slug":"snazzy-skipping-brook","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_0199EksqQu4xvo1bC7YmiUsn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I can see the structure but the actual component data is protobuf-encoded in the Firestore LevelDB. The document paths are:\n- `teamProjects/3d51231e18d88059VtM` \n- `teamProjects/2d9821803uph6`\n- etc.\n\nThese are Firestore document IDs for the project data. However, the actual component tree data is encoded in Firestore's protobuf format within the LevelDB values. Extracting usable SwiftUI component data from this binary format would require:\n1. A Firestore LevelDB parser\n2. Understanding Play's protobuf schema\n\n\n3. Significant reverse engineering\n\nLet me try a more practical approach instead. I could navigate Play via iPhone Mirroring with more precise coordinates, or look for an export feature that generates SwiftUI code directly. I'm also wondering if Play's Mac app has a web interface I can access through the browser, or if I can pull data from the Chrome IndexedDB at createwithplay.com.\n\nActually, I just realized there might be a web version of Play at createwithplay.com that would be much simpler than trying to mirror the app. Let me check if I can access the web editor and my projects directly through the browser.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1278,"cache_read_input_tokens":119551,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1278},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"}},"requestId":"req_011CYasKTjNVmjVWgQag6ZiH","type":"assistant","uuid":"093844e5-47dd-4a18-9368-ee03b086d8de","timestamp":"2026-02-28T17:33: