{"parentUuid":"28ecb769-cc18-4222-aeca-18fdae64a1f2","isSidechain":false,"userType":"external","cwd":"/Users/sa/rh.1","sessionId":"94aabe90-9b2d-48e7-be8c-6bf820a0b6a9","version":"2.1.59","gitBranch":"stage4-release","slug":"snazzy-skipping-brook","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_018fH8WXz3wtwSk8ZXCLyLmD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Same screen, no change. The play button click might not have registered, or it requires a different gesture, or the play button is slightly in a different position.\n\nLet me reconsider the approach. I'm spending a lot of time and tokens on pixel-by-pixel navigation of Play.app through iPhone Mirroring. This is inherently slow and unreliable. Let me step back and think about what's actually productive.\n\nThe user wants to extract Play components before it shuts down (April 20, 2026). We have time. The current approach (iPhone Mirroring → cliclick) is working (the context menu appeared earlier) but it's slow.\n\nLet me try a smarter strategy:\n\n\nInstead of relying on Play's export feature, I can navigate through each screen, screenshot the designs, and manually recreate the SwiftUI code myself — since Play generates standard SwiftUI components like VStack, HStack, and Text anyway, I can reconstruct them from what I see visually.\n\nLet me check the current project first — it only has three mostly empty screens, so the actual components I need are in Mika's Team project. Actually, let me try accessing Play's web editor directly through Chrome instead, since that would be much easier to interact with than trying to find export buttons through screen mirroring.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":631,"cache_read_input_tokens":127458,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":631},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"}},"requestId